    "image_with_text_Ky4pCx": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_jEmg9w": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading_text": "Climate and Average Temperatures",
            "heading_size": "h3",
            "enable_h1_tag": false
          }
        },
        "description_Jk883k": {
          "type": "description",
          "settings": {
            "description_text": "<p>Average monthly temperature figures can have a significant impact on black winter truffle cultivation. As a general guide, seasons need to be clearly defined, but not characterized by extremes.</p><p><strong>Winter Temperatures:</strong> The typical average temperatures for the coldest month in natural truffle areas are between 35-45 degrees Fahrenheit. We believe that areas with warmer winters can be productive as long as the evening temperature falls significantly and light frosts are not uncommon. However, significant and extended freezing of the soil to the depth of the truffles can severely impact the harvest by damaging the fruits. Therefore, areas with very low winter temperatures should be avoided.</p><p><strong>Summer Temperatures: </strong>Truffle orchards require sunlight penetration of the canopy and warm soil temperatures to aid development of the fruiting body over the summer months. Typical average temperatures for the warmest months within production areas range between 62-74 degrees Fahrenheit. High summer temperatures may be managed with mulching, tree densities, row-orientation and irrigation.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_jEmg9w",
        "description_Jk883k"
      ],
      "name": "t:sections.images_with_text.name",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "scheme-1",
        "image_alignment": "middle-xs",
        "image_1": "shopify://shop_images/Winter_vs_summer.jpg",
        "image_1_link": "",
        "image_2_link": "",
